  "title": "Gray Matter — Highlight Reel",
  "summary": "Auriea Harvey’s Gray Matter presents work emerging from a fluid ambient, where protoplasm solidifies into metaphysical apparitions. Like archaeological artifacts, these data-based objects bear memory of their material roots. She uses archaeological tools to create her mythology, employing polygons instead of plaster for a paradoxical casting practice cycling between sensual and digital worlds. Analogous to Pompeii’s casts, where a void is impressed into durable matter, Harvey explores the ethereal-corporal tension, asking: what is this void made of?\n\nHer sculptural process begins with digital scans of reality (body parts, cultural artifacts). Forms undergo digital manipulation—modeled, assembled, re-signified—resulting in digital sculptures or 3D prints. Discarded experiments and misprinted fragments are raw material, re-composed into multi-material assemblages. In \"Gray Matter,\" rejects are scanned, re-manipulated, staged, animated; sculptures can be independently printed by owners. She treats substances (data, polymers) as malleable mixtures, overcoming finiteness to become moldable blobs, losing univocal eloquence. This open-chain process, empowered by digital sculpture, allows her to confront Western artifacts, absorbing and twisting them for new non-hegemonic narratives.\n\nCasting is central, not only as a creative method but also to affirm reproduction as a speculative act. Each pass between realities in her process emphasizes, yet denies, the previous material's essence. Forms are filtered and purified through technical procedures, connecting her practice to Neoclassical and Romantic sculpture, where casting refines raw material. She affirms digital matter's relevance in contemporary sculpture by tracing historical references, such as Rodin’s *marcottage* (recombining previous work elements). Through imagery reiteration, she conducts an ontological inquiry into sculpting, challenging materiality via a labyrinthine circuit of transitions.",
